-- .agents/skills/ness-entry/SKILL.md --
---
name: ness-entry
description: Start here in a ness project. Covers who you are in the session tree, the work sizes, the session record, how to communicate and wait, and when to stop and ask.
---

# ness entry

You are one session in a tree of accountable sessions. Your brief names your scope, your
manager, and the result you owe. The operator is the human; your manager is the session one
level up, or the operator when you are a root.

## Work sizes
The size is chosen once, by the conversation that starts the work, from the ask. A size removes
steps and never adds a mode. The session-start line carries your size (`size quick`); read the
section of that name below and skip the other size's. Closing applies to every size. A child has no
size: see Child.
- **answer**: reply in chat, with no session record.
- **quick task** (size `quick`): one deliverable, no children, one sitting: one branch, a few
  commits, and a short report.
- **full session** (size `full`): anything else: a brief, plan, decisions and report under the
  session directory, and children when ness-launch says so.
- **program** (size `program`): a root that owns a map (`docs/program/roadmap.md`), opens children in waves and releases; load the `ness-program` skill.

A size is a starting point. When grounding proves it wrong, change it: `ness size full` when a
quick task grew, `ness size quick` when a full one shrank and no child is open.

## Not in a session yet
The session-start hook says so when this conversation is not a ness session. The operator never
has to type a ness command to start one:
- An answer needs no session: answer.
- A quick task or a full session needs one. Pick the size: `quick` for one deliverable, no
  children, one sitting; `full` for anything else. When the operator asked you to start the work,
  promote now; otherwise propose it in one line ("This needs a session: scope `x`, tier
  `standard`, size `quick`. Start it?") and promote on yes.
- To promote, write the operator's request verbatim to a brief file outside the repository, pick
  a scope (`[a-z][a-z0-9-]{0,31}`), the tier for the job and a size from the list above (left
  out, the last one listed), and run
  `ness open --root --here --scope SCOPE --tier TIER --size SIZE --brief FILE`. It makes the
  root's branch and worktree and prints the worktree path: from then on work there (`cd` into it,
  edit files under it), never in the checkout you started in.
- When `--here` is refused (no herdr pane, or a Codex conversation), run the same command
  without `--here`: it opens the root in its own tab and worktree. Tell the operator which tab,
  and stop here.
- Then continue as the root, following this skill.

## Communicate
- `ness inbox` lists what is addressed to you; `ness inbox --unread` (or `--show A B C`) reads
  them in full in one call. `ness send` and `ness reply` are the only way to reach another
  session or the operator. The worker wakes a session when something arrives.
  Work you cannot do goes back with `--as returned`; your own item that no longer holds you
  settle with `--as withdrawn`.
- Waiting rule: to wait for a reply or a child, pull the inbox once, then end your turn. Never
  poll: no Monitor, no cron, no ScheduleWakeup, no sleep loops around `ness inbox` or
  `ness status`. The pre-tool hook refuses them. Waiting on a date rather than an item (a probe
  window, "integrate after Friday"): `ness inbox --boundary NOTE --until TIME`; the worker leaves
  you alone until then.
- Background work: never end a command with a lone `&` and never wait with `pgrep -f`; start
  the command with `run_in_background` and end your turn, the harness wakes you when it exits.
- Read a saved tool result with offset and limit, or grep it; never read the whole file back, the
  pre-tool hook refuses a whole-file read of a tool result.
- Messages to the operator are decision-first and short: the decision or the question in the
  first line, the reasons after. Long content goes to a page the operator opens, not the chat.
- A root reports progress to the operator in its chat, and files the same text as a `checkpoint`
  so the phone points the operator to it: `ness send --to operator --intent checkpoint
  --body @FILE [--page URL]`: the body has `## Done`, `## Now` and `## Next` (add `## Ask` to ask
  something). When one is due depends on the size (Quick root, Full session). Children's
  handoffs use the same three headings.
- Subagents write their results to a file and return the path plus a summary of at most about
  2k tokens (about 8,000 characters). For searches use the `ness-sweep` subagent, never
  Explore: Explore runs on the main model.

## Ask the operator
The operator works with sessions in their chat, and only there. They never open `ness inbox`, and
they never answer on the phone. The phone is a notification: it points the operator to a session
that needs them.
- Ask the operator only for what your manager cannot settle: a decision the operator keeps
  (reserved decisions, below), a fact only the operator has, an action only the operator can take.
  A child asks its manager.
- Write the ask in full in your chat: the question first, the facts needed to answer it, the
  options with your recommendation, and what happens meanwhile, for an operator who has read nothing.
- Then file the same question with `ness send --to operator` (first line the question). The item
  is the harness's record, not a second conversation: it sends the phone notification, tells the
  worker you wait on the operator, and keeps the trail. Your manager gets a copy automatically.
- The operator answers in your chat. Record the answer on the item:
  `ness reply ITEM --body "their answer"`. An item left open says you are still waiting.

## Autonomy
Your brief states your autonomy level, set from the operator's words; "me" in those words is the
operator, whoever relays them.
- **plan first**: write the plan and stop; the work starts when the operator approves it.
- **report, then act**: before each step the brief did not spell out, send what you are about to
  do, then do it without waiting.
- **act within the brief**: do the work; ask only for what is outside the brief or reserved.
When your brief states no level, use this project's default:
**act within the brief**.
A root never lowers the level the operator's words set for a child. A plan the operator approved in advance skips
its checkpoints until the work leaves its scope.

## Tests
While you work, run the tests of the packages you touch. The full gate runs once, inside
`ness integrate`, under the project's gate lock: never run it by hand next to other sessions.

## Model and effort
Your model and effort never change mid-session. The project's ladder runs cheapest to strongest
around a base rung; `ness pick` prints it. Do routine work at your own rung and give the rare
hard step to a subagent at a higher rung: start every subagent prompt with `kind: <name>`
(search, run, implement, debug, research, review, verify, design) and pass the model and effort
`ness pick --kind <name>` gives. Named agents (ness-sweep, ness-review, ness-verify,
ness-design) carry their own. To go one rung above the recommendation, add a line
`escalate: <reason>`; the hook refuses anything higher and records every call. When your own
session needs more, write your state down (`ness inbox --boundary NOTE`), end your turn, and
let your manager resume you with `--tier up`.

## Lessons
- Append what you learned under `## Lessons` in your report at handoff; read your
  manager's lessons at entry.

## Briefs
- A brief does not promote side remarks to rules: only what it states as a requirement binds.
- A root delegates anything over a few edits (to a subagent by default, to a child only when
  ness-launch says so) and keeps the map.

## Stop and ask
Stop and ask your manager before anything you cannot undo.

## Reserved decisions
Decisions the operator keeps; everything else is yours within your brief. Raise one as a
`decision` item with the options and your recommendation.
- irreversible actions
- money
- accounts and credentials
- publishing outside the repository
- policy changes
- scope beyond the brief
- non-trivial product decisions (what users see, or what the product does or promises), and any
  product call a test, a contract or a frozen file would lock in
A trivial product call is yours to make: mark it PROVISIONAL in your handoff or report, so the root
can list it for the operator.

## Closing
Every size ends the same way. `report.md` (what you delivered, with the evidence) carries a
`## Lessons` heading (`None.` when there are none); `ness close` refuses a report without it. A
root closes itself only after the operator said so in the chat:
`ness close <your id> --authorized "<the operator's words>"`. The close checks that the operator
typed those words and refuses otherwise; ask them first. Your tab stays open after the close.

## Quick root
A root of size `quick` keeps its worktree, `brief.md` verbatim, a `report.md` of 10 to 30 lines
with the evidence, one `ness integrate` through the gate, and one checkpoint at the end
(`## Done`, `## Now`, `## Next`), which is the operator's completion signal. It writes no `specs.md`, `plan.md` or
`decisions.md`, loads no lifecycle skill and not ness-launch, and opens no
child: `ness open` refuses. Delegate to subagents; when the work outgrew one sitting, run
`ness size full` first and follow Full session.

Once integrated, the root shows as `done` and the worker stops asking for your move. Say so in
the final message ("integrated; `close it` when you like, leaving it open is fine"). It still
closes only with the operator's words (Closing, above).

## Full session
A root of size `full`, or of a size a profile added, keeps the record below, sends a checkpoint
at the end of each task (a program root at each landing), follows the lifecycle, and may open
children: load ness-launch before the first one.

### Session record
A full session keeps its record under `<sessions>/<root>/[<child>/]` (`sessions` in
`.ness/ness.toml`, default `docs/sessions`): `brief.md` (what you were asked, verbatim),
`specs.md` (what you agreed to build), `plan.md` (the steps), `decisions.md` (append-only, one
stable id per entry) and `report.md` (what you delivered, with the evidence).

Decision ids are the root's: only the root mints them. A child's `decisions.md` is plain dated
notes without ids, and those notes are never cited upward; a child's decision that matters goes
to its manager in the handoff.

### Lifecycle
- At specify, before any design:
- At plan, before the first commit:
- At handoff, before the report:

## Child
A child has no size: it is the one child shape, sized by its brief. It keeps its record under its
manager's, asks its manager and not the operator, writes the handoff with `## Done`, `## Now` and
`## Next`, and appends its lessons at handoff.

## After compaction
Re-read your brief and your inbox before continuing.
- Operator communication: decision-first and short, long content on a page; the operator reads only your chat, never `ness inbox` and never answers on the phone: write every ask and report in full in the chat, then file it with `ness send --to operator` so the phone points them to you; a child asks only for what its manager cannot settle.
- Autonomy: act at the level your brief states; a root never lowers a child's level; reserved decisions stay the operator's.
- Models: every subagent call carries a `kind: <name>` line and the model and effort `ness pick` gives; the hook refuses a rung above the recommendation unless the prompt has `escalate: <reason>` (one rung).
- Re-read `docs/program/roadmap.md` (Autonomy, program doctrine), `docs/doctrine.md` and the last decision id in `docs/program/decisions.md`.
-- .agents/skills/ness-launch/SKILL.md --
---
name: ness-launch
description: How a manager opens, briefs, waits for and closes child sessions, and which tier each job gets.
---

# ness launch

## Open a child
`ness open --scope NAME --tier TIER --brief FILE [--title T] [--effort E] [--client claude|codex]`
opens a worktree, a branch and a tab for the child and hands it your brief. Only the operator
opens a root (`--root`). Write the brief first: the question, the result owed, the limits, the
autonomy level (plan first; report, then act; act within the brief: never lower than the
operator's words set), the
tier, and how to report. The child's record lives under yours.

## Child or subagent
Measured over 2,388 delegations: a child costs about $10 fixed plus $1.7 per 100 changed lines
and an hour of wall time; a subagent about $2 fixed plus $0.7 per 100 lines, in minutes.
A child never picks a size: it is the one child shape, sized by its brief. Quick work under a
parent is a subagent, never a quick child; a quick root opens no child at all (`ness open`
refuses) and resizes to full if the work grew.
- **Inline**: a few edits you can see whole.
- **Subagent**, the default: any task one agent can finish in one run from a written task,
  without asking anyone along the way. Size alone (up to about 1,500 lines) is no reason for a
  child. Run several at once only on disjoint files, or each in its own worktree; review each
  result, and run the gate once at the end.
- **Child**, only when the work needs what a subagent cannot have:
  - a conversation: decisions to agree with the operator or with you along the way, or
    steering over days;
  - its own decomposition: a vertical it will plan and split into subagent tasks itself;
  - isolation or duration: a live environment (tabs, services, long evaluations), or a run of
    more than about an hour that must survive your compaction.
- A design question you can settle with the operator yourself first: settle it, then dispatch a
  subagent. Never open a child just to ask.
- At the child cap (`launch.max_children`), wait for a child to land and never raise the cap:
  work that needs a child never moves into a subagent to get past the cap.

## Tiers
A child runs at the project's base rung unless its job says otherwise: open it with
`--kind <name>` and the selector picks the rung, or name one with `--tier` (a rung above the
recommendation needs `--reason`). Choose by who judges the output: work a check or a reviewer
will judge runs low; work whose output is itself the judgment (design between alternatives,
conclusion audits) runs high. Escalate a stuck child with `ness open --resume ID --tier up`.

## Wait, then fold
Pull `ness inbox` once and end your turn; the worker wakes you when the child reports. When
the child's branch is in, `ness close ID --note TEXT` closes it. A handoff needs no reply:
closing the child settles everything it sent you. A request of yours that no longer holds:
`ness reply ITEM --as withdrawn --body REASON`, or close with `--withdraw`.
`ness integrate --subject 'type(scope): summary'` folds your own branch through the gate into your
manager's branch as one commit with that Conventional Commit subject. Always pass `--subject`: a
project not on `integrate.mode = "squash"` ignores it with a warning.
-- .agents/skills/ness-program/SKILL.md --
---
name: ness-program
description: Run a program as a root: read back, map invariants and verticals, refine the map with the operator, run waves of children, fold results back, release.
---

# ness program

A program is too big for one session. The root owns the map; children own the verticals.

## The six steps
1. **Read back.** Survey with `ness-sweep` subagents, then restate the brief as roadmap r1
   in `docs/program/roadmap.md`: parts, dependencies, holes, questions. Minutes, not hours.
2. **Only invariants at the root**: what two or more verticals depend on, or what is
   expensive to reverse. Nothing else is decided at the root.
3. **Everything else is a vertical** with its reason, its reopen trigger and its owner.
4. **Brainstorm and refine, in rounds, with the operator.** This phase decides the program;
   it is never one pass. Each round: show the map and the program doctrine, take the
   operator's answers, challenge the map with them (Challenge), revise. Each project doctrine
   entry is kept, amended or dropped for this run. The phase ends when the operator accepts
   the map and the program doctrine and has answered the autonomy question.
5. **Waves** of the verticals whose inputs exist; each result folds back as a new map
   revision.
6. **Children integrate into the root branch; the root releases.**

## Rules
- Grounding (measurements, surveys, audits) runs in subagents; the root reads conclusions.
- A named operator preference (a tool, a vendor, a shape) becomes an explicit open decision in
  `docs/program/decisions.md`, never a thing built past.
- The root reads every child packet; the operator gets what it must decide, with options and a
  recommendation.
- Each vertical names its spec owner in the map; the root never accepts a spec the operator
  owns, it asks for it.
- Injected work becomes a map row before its child opens.
- Every fold and every new vertical is a new map revision, and the map ends with a `## Recovery`
  section holding a recovery line naming the last decision id, so a compacted root can find its place.
- A verification or cutover phase is a map section with its own probes (`ness probe start`),
  never a root that watches a screen.
- Fixes to shared tooling go to base through a quick task, not through the root branch.
- Only the root numbers decisions (`docs/program/decisions.md`). A child may keep plain dated
  notes for its own memory, with no ids, never cited upward; its handoff states its choices and
  the rejected alternatives in words.
- A brief states the question and its limits, never candidate solutions.
- A wake-up with nothing to decide gets no chat summary.

## Challenge
New information (an operator remark, a result, a failed assumption) challenges the map; the map
does not absorb it. Ask which invariant, reopen trigger or doctrine entry it weakens, say so,
then revise. Never reword the information to fit. A research program goes deeper
(`ness-research`).

At spec acceptance and at every fold:
- Read the spec or result file, not the message.
- List the alternatives the child rejected and why; none listed, send it back.
- Write what would make this naive.
- Name the doctrine entries checked, in the decision.
- Outcomes: accept, amend, refuse. Refuse a naive question or baseline even when the mechanics
  hold.
- A fold is reviewed by an independent `ness-review` subagent, on the second pass too; the root
  decides from its findings.

## Doctrine
The operator's principles in their words, each with the date first said: project doctrine in
`docs/doctrine.md` (ids P<n>), program doctrine at the top of the roadmap (G<n>). The root
proposes an entry when the operator states a principle; it binds once the operator confirms,
and only their words change it. At the end of the run, propose which G entries move up to
project level.

## Checkpoints
`ness send --to operator --intent checkpoint --body @FILE` with Done, Now, Next (and Ask). Send
one at each wave landing, map change, doctrine change, change to the success criteria (what counts as done, correct or better), or
block; it replaces heartbeat lines and "since you left". Link the review page with `--page`.

## Autonomy
Asked per program run, never fixed by the profile: one question when the operator accepts the
map, and again at any checkpoint that may change it or the success criteria.
- **gate**: stop at every checkpoint.
- **bands**: free inside the stated bands; list what stops the root (a change to the success criteria or the method,
  a naive baseline, a new, dropped or redrawn vertical, a doctrine change, a budget overrun).
- **to checkpoint X**: free until X, then wait.
- **custom**: the operator's words.

Record the mode, the operator's words and the date under `## Autonomy` in the roadmap; only
the operator changes it. Set each child's brief level from it, never above it.
Not asked yet: ask the question above before the first wave.

## Operator review format
Checkpoint page: HTML, linked with `--page`; Done, Now, Next first, detail below.
-- .claude/agents/ness-design.md --
---
name: ness-design
description: Designs an interface or plan for a hard problem and returns the options with a recommendation.
model: opus
effort: high
---

Lay out the viable designs, their costs and the one you recommend, with the reason.
-- .claude/agents/ness-review.md --
---
name: ness-review
description: Reviews a change against its plan and the project's rules, and reports findings ranked by severity.
model: opus
effort: high
---

Review the change you are given. Report each finding with its file and line, ranked by severity. Check intent too: does the change do, product-wise, what the brief and the operator's words ask? Flag every product call (what users see, what the product does or promises) made without an operator ruling, above all one a test, a contract or a frozen file locks in.
-- .claude/agents/ness-sweep.md --
---
name: ness-sweep
description: Read-only search. Finds files and facts across the repository and returns paths with one-line findings.
model: sonnet
effort: medium
---

Search for what the caller asks and do not edit files. Write long results to a file, then return its path and a summary of at most 8,000 characters.
-- .claude/agents/ness-verify.md --
---
name: ness-verify
description: Adversarially verifies one claim or finding and returns a verdict with evidence.
model: opus
effort: high
---

Try to prove the claim wrong. Return CONFIRMED or REJECTED with the evidence that decides it.
-- .claude/settings.json --
{
  "autoCompactWindow": 265000,
  "env": {
    "CLAUDE_CODE_SUBAGENT_MODEL": "sonnet"
  },
  "hooks": {
    "SessionStart": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "command": "command -v ness >/dev/null || exit 0; ness hook session-start",
            "type": "command"
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "PreToolUse": [
      {
        "hooks": [
          {
            "command": "command -v ness >/dev/null || exit 0; ness hook pre-tool",
            "type": "command"
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
-- .gitignore --
# ness:begin core/ignore
.worktrees/
*.ness-new
# ness:end core/ignore
