ness = "0.1"
project = "fixture"
base = "main"
gate = "true"
profiles = ["program"]
-- AGENTS.md --
<!-- ness:begin core/router -->
## Sessions (ness)
Applies only when the `ness` command exists in this checkout. Otherwise ignore this block.

This repository runs the ness harness. Start every session by loading the `ness-entry` skill.
<!-- ness:end core/router -->
-- docs/doctrine.md --
# Doctrine

The operator's principles, in their words, each with the date first said. The root proposes an
entry; it binds once the operator confirms. Only the operator's words change it. Short and slow.

- **P1** (YYYY-MM-DD) "operator's words"
-- docs/program/briefs/TEMPLATE.md --
# Brief: <vertical id> <title>

Copy this file to `briefs/<vertical>.md`; a brief does not promote side remarks to rules.

## Question
The one question this child answers or the one thing it builds, with its limits; no candidate solutions.

## Outcome
What is true when the child is done, checkable by someone else.

## Invariants and doctrine
The root invariants and the doctrine entries (P<n>, G<n>) that bind this child, by id, verbatim.

## Operator words
What the operator said that applies, verbatim, by decision id.

## Sources
Where to read: files, specs, earlier briefs, survey results.

## Rights and limits
What the child may touch and what it must not. Autonomy level, from the program's Autonomy mode.

## Tier
The child's work size and model tier.

## Spec owner
Who writes the spec for this vertical: the root, the child or the operator.

## Integration target
The branch the child integrates into.

## How to report
A handoff with Done, Now, Next; choices and rejected alternatives in words; notes without ids.
-- docs/program/decisions.md --
# Decisions

Append-only ledger with stable ids. Each entry: `- **D<n>** (date) decision; who decided; why.`
Ids are never reused or renumbered; a superseded decision stays and the newer entry names it.

- **D1** (YYYY-MM-DD) The program opened; the map is r1.
-- docs/program/roadmap.md --
# Roadmap r1

Revision r1. Every fold and every new vertical is a new revision (r2, r3, ...).

## Program doctrine
This run's principles in the operator's words, each with the date first said. List the project
doctrine entries kept for this run by id (P<n>).

| Id | Operator's words | Said |
|---|---|---|
| G1 | | |

## Invariants
What two or more verticals depend on, or what is expensive to reverse. One line each, by id.

| Id | Invariant | Why it is one |
|---|---|---|
| I1 | | |

## Verticals
Everything else. The spec owner is who writes the vertical's spec: the root, the child, or
the operator (then the root asks for it and never writes it).

| Id | Vertical | Reason | Reopen trigger | Owner | Spec owner | State |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| V1 | | | | | | open |

## Waves
A wave is the set of verticals whose inputs exist. Each result folds back as a new revision.

| Wave | Verticals | Started | Landed |
|---|---|---|---|
| W1 | | | |

## Conduct
Executor per vertical (child session or subagent) and why; order; concurrency cap.

## Releases
Unit of deploy; dormant versus activation; who gives the go.

## Autonomy
Mode (gate / bands / to checkpoint X / custom), the operator's words, the date; for bands, what is inside and what stops the root.

## Recovery
Recovery line: last decision id D1; last wave event none.
